  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency response for internally displaced persons and affected populations impacted by tropical cyclones in the PhilippinesEmergency response for internally displaced persons and affected populations impacted by tropical cyclones in the Philippines</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since July 2025, the Philippines has been affected by a combination of tropical storms, cyclones, and earthquakes. Tyhoon Tino (international name Kalmaegi) and Super Typhoon Uwan (international name Fung-wong) made landfall in November, causing widespread damage to houses, infrastructures and livelihoods, affecting over 18 million people and displacing over 2.3 million persons.Through this project, IOM is mitigating the adverse effects of tropical cyclones, particularly typhoon Tino/Kalmaegi and Uwan/Fung-wong, as well as the compounded impacts of additional disasters in the target areas, on the most vulnerable, affected and/or displaced communities, with a focus on Negros Occidental, Negros Oriental (NIR), and Southern Leyte (Region 8). In close coordination with local authorities, the Humanitarian Country Team and affected communities, IOM is responding to the urgent needs for emergency shelter and Non-Food Items (NFIs), and Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) to enable populations to access safe living conditions and deliver immediate, life-saving assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
